"The stack is appropriate to the problem because the domain is defined by rules rather than by data volume. Marks computation, pass determination and credit accounting are rule-heavy and must be identical on every surface that reports them, which argues for a statically typed, modular server in which the calculators are shared components rather than replicated expressions in a client or a report writer. The relational model is not incidental: roll numbers are unique within an academic year rather than globally, a class context is the tuple of branch, semester, scheme and academic year, and one class teacher per class context is a uniqueness constraint rather than a convention. An object-relational mapper keeps these invariants expressible and checkable, and the compound unique constraint on the mark idempotency key doubles as the index that the per-student result query traverses. The queue exists for a reason that has nothing to do with scale: file parsing and report generation are operations whose failure must be isolated from the request that requested them, and a persisted job with a pollable status gives the operator a durable record of what was submitted and what became of it. The hosted services were chosen so that no local infrastructure is required to evaluate the system, while the local-disk fallback for storage and the null-tolerant cache mean that a missing external service degrades the system rather than disabling it.\n\nThe repository is organised as a monorepo. A single working tree holds the backend service, the browser client, the background worker process and two shared packages, one carrying shared type definitions and the other shared constants such as role names and permission keys. The shared packages exist because the role and permission vocabularies are referenced by the seed, by the server-side authorisation layer and by the client-side screen-capability resolver; duplicating them would allow the three to drift, which is precisely the class of defect the earlier version of the system suffered from. A task orchestrator coordinates the workspace, encoding the build ordering so that the shared packages are always compiled before the applications that import their built output.",
