"Access control in the system is permission-first rather than role-first. A permission is an atomic ability expressed as a resource and an action, and a role is a named bundle of permissions. There is deliberately no role guard in the route layer: routes declare the permissions they require, and role names appear only in the seed data, in the grant-authority rule and in display labels. A member may hold several roles at once, and the effective permission set is their union. Enforcement requires all of the permissions listed on a route, not any one of them, except in the single case where two distinct rights can legitimately attach to the same operation.\n\nThat exception is instructive. Confirming an extracted upload is performed by the examination cell, whose right covers every offering in the class, and by a teacher, whose right covers only their own allocations for the year. These are different abilities with different reach, so they are deliberately distinct permissions; the route declares a common permission and the service resolves which of the two specific rights the caller holds before deciding how much of the uploaded material that caller may write.\n\nThree further properties matter. First, the route guard, the authorisation service and the client-side screen-capability resolver all read the same single implementation of the effective-permission join, so the three cannot disagree about what a member may do. Second, the effective permissions are cached and the cache is invalidated by a global version counter that any change to a role or a grant increments, so every previously cached set is orphaned at once, in constant time, and a permission change takes effect on the member's next request without a re-login. Third, the cache falls back to the database on a miss and on a cache outage. Returning an implicit \"allow\" on a miss would silently disable every permission check in the system, which is the worst failure mode an authorisation cache can exhibit; the implementation therefore fails closed and reads through to the database.",
