"Isolation between departments is achieved through a scope-resolution rule applied to every read and write. A member with at least one branch-bearing role assignment is branch-scoped, and every read is filtered to the set of branches named by those assignments. A member whose assignments all name no branch is college-scoped. The scope is read from the database on each request rather than embedded in the session token, because a person can hold roles in several branches and the union of those assignments, not any single one, decides what they see.\n\nTwo deliberate choices distinguish this rule from a naive implementation. First, the scope is a set rather than a single identifier: a member scoped to two departments who names neither still sees only those two, whereas a single-identifier implementation would drop the filter and return the whole college. Second, a branch-scoped member who names a branch outside their scope is refused rather than silently redirected. A silent redirect would mask a defect in the calling interface and would give the caller a result that appears to satisfy a request they were not entitled to make; refusal makes the mismatch visible. Scope is always derived from the caller and is never accepted as a request parameter. A related distinction is drawn between a scope that covers the whole college and a scope that matches nothing: the first is a legitimate administrative position resolved to an unfiltered query, while the second is not a broader permission but an absence of one, and is treated as an explicit refusal. Collapsing the two would convert a member who has lost every branch assignment into a member who can see everything.",
