"The calculators return full precision. No rounding is performed during computation; rounding is applied only when a value is displayed, so a total that appears to be 49.5 is in fact carried as 49.5 through every subsequent step. The calculators also re-derive the summary columns that a printed statement itself carries — total marks, percentage and grade-point average — rather than trusting the printed values, because the re-derivation is the entire purpose of the exercise.\n\nThe governing scheme is selected per offering rather than configured globally. Each subject offering belongs to a class context that includes a scheme, and the calculator strategy is chosen from the scheme on the record. This matters because a single academic year legitimately contains students on more than one curriculum revision, and a global setting would evaluate all of them by one rule. A single calculator interface with three strategies behind it allows every surface that reports results — the result view, the report writer, the dashboard, the class overview and the topper lists — to consume the same evaluation without reimplementing it.",
